.TH "GREPNET" "1" "2026" "grepnet 5.0.0" "User Commands"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
grepnet - Network-aware grep for CIDR blocks, IP ranges, and network
addresses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]grepnet\f[R] [\f[I]OPTION\f[R]\&...]
\f[I]PATTERN\f[R] [\f[I]FILE\f[R]\&...]
.PP
\f[B]grepnet\f[R] [\f[I]OPTION\f[R]\&...]
\f[B]-f\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN_FILE\f[R] [\f[I]FILE\f[R]\&...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
grepnet searches for network addresses in text files using network
algebra instead of substring matching.
A pattern like \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R] will match any IP address, CIDR
block, subnet mask, or IP range that falls within that
network\[em]regardless of format.
.PP
This makes grepnet ideal for:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Network auditing\f[R]: Find all references to a network across
multiple files and formats
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Configuration review\f[R]: Locate which systems/applications
reference specific networks
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Security analysis\f[R]: Identify networks referenced in logs,
configs, and documentation
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Network extraction\f[R]: Extract and consolidate all networks
mentioned in files
.SS Core Concept: Network Matching
.PP
Unlike grep which does text matching, grepnet understands network
topology.
These all represent the same /24 network:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
192.0.2.0/24             (CIDR)
192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0  (Subnet mask)
192.0.2.0 0.0.0.255      (Inverse mask)
192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255    (IP range)
192.0.2.0-255            (Short-range: octet-based notation)
192.0.2.1                (Single IP within the network)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
A single \f[V]grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq]\f[R] command finds all of
these.
.SH QUICK START
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Find any reference to a network (pattern can be any format)
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt            # CIDR
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0\[aq] config.txt # Subnet mask
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\[aq] config.txt   # IP range
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.1\[aq] config.txt                # Single IP

# Search from stdin
echo \[dq]server 192.0.2.1\[dq] | grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq]

# Search multiple files recursively
grepnet -r \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] /etc/

# Show line numbers and only matching networks
grepnet -n -o \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt

# Extract all networks from a file
grepnet --extract config.txt

# Consolidate overlapping networks
grepnet --consolidate networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.SH PATTERN AND FILE INPUT
.TP
\f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
A single network address in any supported format.
A pattern can be a CIDR block (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R]), an IP
range (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\f[R]), a subnet mask (e.g.,
\f[V]192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0\f[R]), or a single bare IP address (e.g.,
\f[V]192.0.2.1\f[R]).
The tool automatically recognizes the format.
You need only one pattern to search; use
\f[V]-P\f[R]/\f[V]--pattern\f[R] for additional patterns if needed.
See \[lq]Multiple Patterns\[rq] below for how to specify more than one.
.TP
\f[I]FILE\f[R]\&...
Files to search.
If omitted, reads from stdin.
.TP
\f[B]-P\f[R], \f[V]--pattern\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Specify additional patterns beyond the first (repeatable for multiple
patterns).
Each pattern can be in any supported format independently.
.TP
\f[B]-f\f[R], \f[V]--patterns-file\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN_FILE\f[R]
Read patterns from file, one per line.
Each line is treated as a separate pattern in any supported format
(repeatable to load multiple pattern files).
.TP
\f[B]-F\f[R], \f[V]--input-file\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Specify input file explicitly (alternative to positional FILE arguments)
.PP
\f[B]Note on pattern flexibility\f[R]: Each pattern is independent and
can be any supported format.
You don\[cq]t need to define every possible format or provide multiple
patterns.
A single pattern like \f[V]192.0.2.1\f[R] or \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R] is
sufficient for most searches.
Patterns with \f[V]-P\f[R] or \f[V]-f\f[R] are purely optional and only
needed when searching for multiple different networks.
.SH MATCH TYPES
.PP
By default, grepnet finds all matches.
Use these flags to filter by relationship type:
.TP
\f[B]-e\f[R], \f[V]--equal\f[R]
Match only networks that are exactly equal
.TP
\f[B]-s\f[R], \f[V]--subset\f[R]
Match only when file network is smaller than pattern (contained within)
.TP
\f[B]-S\f[R], \f[V]--superset\f[R]
Match only when file network is larger than pattern (contains pattern)
.TP
\f[B]-O\f[R], \f[V]--overlap\f[R]
Match only when networks partially overlap
.TP
\f[B]-u\f[R], \f[V]--unmatched\f[R]
Include networks that don\[cq]t match any pattern (for use with other
match type flags)
.TP
\f[B]-v\f[R], \f[V]--invert-match\f[R]
Show lines that do NOT match (inverse logic)
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]: \f[V]grepnet -s \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] data.txt\f[R]
finds all /24 or /25 networks within 192.0.0.0/16
.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]-o\f[R], \f[V]--only-matching\f[R]
Print only the matched networks, not the entire line
.TP
\f[B]-n\f[R], \f[V]--line-number\f[R]
Prefix output with 1-based line numbers
.TP
\f[B]-b\f[R], \f[V]--byte-offset\f[R]
Prefix output with 0-based byte offsets
.TP
\f[B]-H\f[R], \f[V]--with-filename\f[R]
Include filename in output (default when multiple files)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--no-filename\f[B]\f[R]
Suppress filename in output
.TP
\f[B]-T\f[R], \f[V]--initial-tab\f[R]
Insert tab after line number/byte offset for alignment
.TP
\f[B]-c\f[R], \f[V]--count\f[R]
Print count of matching lines instead of lines
.TP
\f[B]-l\f[R], \f[V]--files-with-matches\f[R]
Print only filenames containing matches (useful for scripting)
.TP
\f[B]-L\f[R], \f[V]--files-without-match\f[R]
Print only filenames without matches
.TP
\f[B]-q\f[R], \f[V]--quiet\f[R], \f[V]--silent\f[R]
Suppress output (exit code only)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--label\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]LABEL\f[R]
Display stdin as \f[I]LABEL\f[R] instead of \[lq](standard input)\[rq]
.TP
\f[B]-m\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R], \f[V]--max-count\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R]
Stop searching after \f[I]NUM\f[R] matches
.SH CONTEXT OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]-A\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R], \f[V]--after-context\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R]
Print \f[I]NUM\f[R] lines after each match
.TP
\f[B]-B\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R], \f[V]--before-context\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R]
Print \f[I]NUM\f[R] lines before each match
.TP
\f[B]-C\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R], \f[V]--context\f[R] \f[I]NUM\f[R]
Print \f[I]NUM\f[R] lines before and after each match
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--group-separator\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]SEP\f[R]
Print \f[I]SEP\f[R] between match groups (default: \[lq]\[en]\[rq])
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--no-group-separator\f[B]\f[R]
Suppress group separator
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]:
\f[V]grepnet -C 2 \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt\f[R] shows each
match with 2 lines of context
.SH EXTRACTION AND CONSOLIDATION
.PP
Network extraction and consolidation are two related operations for
analyzing and simplifying network data:
.PP
\f[B]Extraction\f[R] (\f[V]--extract\f[R]): Scans input and finds all
unique network objects (CIDR blocks, IP ranges, subnets, individual
IPs).
Deduplicates by network value (same network in different formats counts
as one).
Useful for discovering what networks are referenced in files,
configurations, or logs.
.PP
\f[B]Consolidation\f[R] (\f[V]--consolidate\f[R]): Merges overlapping
and adjacent networks into larger blocks, reducing complexity.
For example, \f[V]192.0.2.0/25\f[R] and \f[V]192.0.2.128/25\f[R] merge
into \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R].
This simplifies network inventories and highlights coverage gaps.
.PP
\f[B]Gap Filling\f[R] (\f[V]--fill-pct\f[R]): Optional consolidation
enhancement.
Instead of merging only adjacent networks, allows filling small gaps if
the gap is ≤ N% of the combined network size.
Useful for simplifying \[lq]nearly complete\[rq] network ranges.
For example, with \f[V]--fill-pct=25\f[R]: - \f[V]192.0.2.0/25\f[R] and
\f[V]192.0.2.192/26\f[R] (gap of 64 IPs = 25% of /24) merge into
\f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R] - With \f[V]--fill-pct=10\f[R]:
\f[V]192.0.2.0/25\f[R] and \f[V]192.0.2.224/26\f[R] (gap of 32 IPs =
12.5% of /24) do NOT merge (gap exceeds 10%)
.PP
\f[B]Output Formats\f[R]: Extracted/consolidated networks can be output
in multiple formats (CIDR, IP ranges, short-ranges, individual IPs, or
original format).
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--extract\f[B]\f[R]
Extract all unique networks found in input (deduplicated)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--consolidate\f[B]\f[R]
Consolidate overlapping and adjacent networks into larger blocks
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--fill-pct\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Allow consolidation to fill gaps up to N% of network size (only with
\f[V]--consolidate\f[R]; range 0-100)
.IP \[bu] 2
Finds the smallest supernet for each network where gap_ratio ≤ N%
.IP \[bu] 2
Single-pass: fill-pct applies once per extracted network, then networks
merge via \f[V]collapse_addresses()\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
Examples: \f[V]--fill-pct=50\f[R] allows up to 50% gap filling;
\f[V]--fill-pct=0\f[R] disallows any gap
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--show-content\f[B]\f[R]
Include original line content in extract/consolidate output
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--format\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]cidr\f[R]|\f[B]range\f[R]|\f[B]short-range\f[R]|\f[B]ips\f[R]|\f[B]original\f[R]}
Output format for networks (default: \f[B]cidr\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cidr\f[R] - CIDR notation: \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]range\f[R] - Full ranges: \f[V]192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\f[R]
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]short-range\f[R] - Abbreviated ranges: \f[V]192.0.2.0-255\f[R]
(IPv4 only, last octet)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ips\f[R] - Individual IP addresses, one per line (only with
\f[V]--consolidate\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]original\f[R] - Original format as found (not available with
\f[V]--consolidate\f[R])
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract all unique networks
grepnet --extract network_logs.txt

# Consolidate overlapping networks into larger blocks
grepnet --consolidate all_networks.txt

# Consolidate with gap filling (up to 50%)
grepnet --consolidate --fill-pct=50 network_ranges.txt

# Extract networks matching a pattern, output as ranges
grepnet --extract \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] systems.txt --format range

# Consolidate and output individual IPs
grepnet --consolidate --format ips networks.txt

# Consolidate and show which lines contained each network
grepnet --consolidate --show-content config.txt

# Combine fill-pct with IP list output
grepnet --consolidate --fill-pct=50 --format ips network_ranges.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.SH FILTERING
.PP
Filtering allows you to narrow or refine search results by excluding
networks or matches that don\[cq]t meet your criteria.
Two independent filtering approaches:
.PP
\f[B]Text-based filtering\f[R] (\f[V]--exclude-string\f[R]): Excludes
individual network matches whose matched text contains a regex pattern.
Useful for filtering by context (e.g., \[lq]deprecated\[rq],
\[lq]testing\[rq], \[lq]archived\[rq]).
Applied after network matching\[em]a network match is excluded if its
matched text matches the regex.
If a line has no remaining matches after text filtering, the entire line
is removed from output.
.PP
\f[B]Network-based filtering\f[R] (\f[V]--exclude-cidr\f[R]): Excludes
network matches that have specific relationships to excluded CIDR
blocks.
The relationship is determined by \f[V]--exclude-cidr-behaviour\f[R].
Useful for filtering by network hierarchy (e.g., exclude all internal
networks, keep only external references).
.PP
\f[B]Combining filters\f[R]: Text and network filters are independent
and stackable: -
\f[V]--exclude-string \[aq]deprecated\[aq] --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.168.0.0/16\[aq]\f[R]
excludes matches where the text contains \[lq]deprecated\[rq] OR the
network is in 192.168.0.0/16 - Multiple \f[V]--exclude-string\f[R] or
\f[V]--exclude-cidr\f[R] flags accumulate (any match causes exclusion)
.SS Text Filtering
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-string\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Exclude network matches whose matched text matches regex PATTERN
(repeatable)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-strings-file\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load text patterns to exclude from file
.PP
\f[B]Behavior\f[R]: For each network match found, test the matched text
against each regex pattern.
If any match, exclude that result.
If a line\[cq]s last network match is excluded by text filtering, the
entire line is removed from output.
.SS Network Filtering
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-cidr\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]NETWORK\f[R]
Exclude specific networks (repeatable)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-cidrs-file\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load networks to exclude from file
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-cidr-behaviour\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]subset\f[R]|\f[B]subset-strict\f[R]|\f[B]superset\f[R]|\f[B]superset-strict\f[R]|\f[B]equals\f[R]|\f[B]related\f[R]|\f[B]cut\f[R]}
How to match excluded networks (default: \f[B]subset\f[R])
.SS Exclusion Behaviors
.PP
Control how excluded networks are matched:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]subset\f[R] (⊆): Exclude networks contained within excluded CIDR,
including exact matches (default)
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Filtering by network hierarchy (\[lq]exclude this network and
anything inside it\[rq])
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]subset-strict\f[R] (⊂): Exclude networks strictly contained within
excluded CIDR, excluding exact matches
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Filtering all subnets of a network but keeping the parent
network itself
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]superset\f[R] (⊇): Exclude networks that contain excluded CIDR,
including exact matches
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Filtering broader networks (\[lq]exclude networks that
encompass this range\[rq])
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]superset-strict\f[R] (⊃): Exclude networks strictly containing
excluded CIDR, excluding exact matches
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Filtering broader networks but keeping networks at the same
level
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]equals\f[R]: Exclude only exact matches
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Blacklisting specific networks
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]related\f[R]: Exclude any network that touches, contains, or is
contained by excluded CIDR
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Filtering all networks touching a specific range (strictest
exclusion)
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]cut\f[R]: Remove excluded network and return non-overlapping
remainder blocks
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
Use when: Carving out excluded ranges and returning only the gaps (e.g.,
whitelist by exclusion)
.RE
.SS Exclusion Examples
.PP
\f[B]Scenario 1: Filter internal networks from results\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Exclude all private networks (default subset behavior)
grepnet --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] --exclude-cidr \[aq]172.16.0.0/12\[aq] \[rs]
  --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.168.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] all_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Scenario 2: Keep only subnets of a network, not parent
networks\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Find subnets of 192.0.0.0/16, but exclude 192.0.0.0/16 itself
grepnet --extract --exclude-cidr-behaviour subset-strict \[rs]
  --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Scenario 3: Whitelist by exclusion (find only gaps)\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Find networks NOT in allowed ranges (carve out allowed space)
grepnet --extract --consolidate --exclude-cidr-behaviour cut \[rs]
  --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.168.0.0/16\[aq] all_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Cut Behavior Details
.PP
The \f[V]cut\f[R] behavior is unique: instead of filtering out matched
networks, it returns the complement\[em]the \[lq]gaps\[rq] left after
removing excluded networks.
Useful for: - Finding unaccounted network space - Whitelist-based
filtering (define what\[cq]s allowed, get everything else) - Network
carving (subdivide networks by removing sections)
.PP
Example:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Input networks: 10.0.0.0/8
# Exclude: 10.10.0.0/16
# Result: 10.0.0.0/22, 10.0.4.0/22, ..., 10.10.0.0/16 (gap), ..., 10.255.0.0/22
grepnet --extract --exclude-cidr-behaviour cut --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.10.0.0/16\[aq] \[rs]
  --format cidr networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.SH FILE DISCOVERY
.PP
File discovery controls which files are scanned when searching
directories.
This is critical for large directory trees where you need to focus on
specific file types or exclude large backup/archive sections.
.PP
\f[B]Default behavior\f[R]: When no include/exclude filters specified,
all files in the directory tree are scanned.
.PP
\f[B]Include patterns\f[R] (\f[V]--include\f[R]): Whitelist files
matching glob patterns.
Only files matching at least one include pattern are scanned.
Can be specified multiple times (OR\[cq]d together).
.PP
\f[B]Exclude patterns\f[R] (\f[V]--exclude\f[R]): Blacklist files
matching glob patterns.
Files matching any exclude pattern are skipped.
Can be specified multiple times.
Applied after include filtering.
.PP
\f[B]Directory-level exclusion\f[R] (\f[V]--exclude-dir\f[R]): Faster
alternative to \f[V]--exclude\f[R].
Skips entire directories without traversing them.
More efficient than glob patterns on deep directory trees.
.PP
\f[B]Symlink handling\f[R]: - \f[V]-r\f[R] (default): Don\[cq]t follow
symlinks (safer, faster) - \f[V]-R\f[R]: Follow symlinks (can cause
infinite loops if circular links exist)
.PP
\f[B]Glob pattern syntax\f[R]: Uses standard glob wildcards: -
\f[V]*\f[R] matches anything except \f[V]/\f[R] (doesn\[cq]t cross
directory boundaries) - \f[V]?\f[R] matches single character -
\f[V][abc]\f[R] matches character class - \f[V]**\f[R] matches any path
including \f[V]/\f[R] (if supported)
.PP
\f[B]Example precedence\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Input: /var/log/2024/archived/app.log
--include \[aq]*.log\[aq] → Matches ✓
--exclude \[aq]*/archived/*\[aq] → Excluded ✗
Result: File NOT scanned
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]-r\f[R], \f[V]--recursive\f[R]
Search directories recursively (don\[cq]t follow symlinks; faster,
safer)
.TP
\f[B]-R\f[R], \f[V]--dereference-recursive\f[R]
Search directories recursively (follow symlinks; can cause infinite
loops)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--include\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]GLOB\f[R]
Include only files matching glob pattern (repeatable; patterns OR\[cq]d
together)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]GLOB\f[R]
Exclude files matching glob pattern (repeatable; patterns OR\[cq]d
together)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-from\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Load exclude patterns from file, one per line
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--exclude-dir\f[B]\f[R] \f[I]PATTERN\f[R]
Exclude directories matching glob pattern (repeatable; doesn\[cq]t
traverse excluded dirs)
.SS Common Patterns
.PP
\f[B]Configuration auditing\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Search only .conf and .yaml files
grepnet -r --include \[aq]*.conf\[aq] --include \[aq]*.yaml\[aq] \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] /etc/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Log analysis\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Search logs excluding old archives and backups
grepnet -r --exclude-dir \[aq]archive\[aq] --exclude-dir \[aq]backup\[aq] \[rs]
  --exclude \[aq]*.gz\[aq] --exclude \[aq]*.zip\[aq] \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] /var/log/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Backup exclusion\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Search everywhere except backups and cache
grepnet -r --exclude-dir \[aq].git\[aq] --exclude-dir \[aq]__pycache__\[aq] \[rs]
  --exclude-dir \[aq]node_modules\[aq] \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] /home/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Whitelist-based scanning\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Only scan source code files
grepnet -r --include \[aq]*.py\[aq] --include \[aq]*.js\[aq] --include \[aq]*.go\[aq] \[rs]
  \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] src/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Performance tip\f[R]: Use \f[V]--exclude-dir\f[R] instead of
\f[V]--exclude\f[R] for large directories\[em]it skips traversal
entirely instead of checking each file.
.SH PARSING CONTROL
.PP
Parsing control allows you to restrict which network formats grepnet
accepts.
This is useful for:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Enforcing consistency\f[R]: Require all networks in a specific
format (e.g., only CIDR)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Reducing false positives\f[R]: Disable formats that might be
ambiguous or commonly mistyped
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Validation\f[R]: Ensure input conforms to expected structure before
processing
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Format migration\f[R]: Accept input in old format, require output
in new format
.PP
\f[B]Key concept\f[R]: Each format type can be enabled/disabled
independently for patterns (search terms) and input (data being
searched).
This allows asymmetric rules like \[lq]accept any format in input, but
only CIDR in patterns.\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Default behavior\f[R]: All formats accepted everywhere
(\f[V]both\f[R]).
This provides maximum flexibility but may catch unintended matches.
.SS Format Options
.PP
Each format flag accepts one of four modes:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]pattern\f[R]: Accept format only in search patterns (via
\f[V]-P\f[R], \f[V]-f\f[R], or arguments)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]input\f[R]: Accept format only in data files being searched
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]both\f[R]: Accept format in both patterns and input (default)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]none\f[R]: Disable format entirely
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--parse-cidr\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]pattern\f[R]|\f[B]input\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Accept CIDR notation (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R]; default:
\f[B]both\f[R])
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--parse-netmask\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]pattern\f[R]|\f[B]input\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Accept subnet mask notation (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0\f[R];
default: \f[B]both\f[R])
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--parse-hostmask\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]pattern\f[R]|\f[B]input\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Accept inverse/host mask notation (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.0 0.0.0.255\f[R];
default: \f[B]both\f[R])
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--parse-ranges\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]pattern\f[R]|\f[B]input\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Accept IP range notation (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\f[R];
default: \f[B]both\f[R])
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--parse-bare-ip\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]pattern\f[R]|\f[B]input\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Accept single IP addresses without CIDR notation (e.g.,
\f[V]192.0.2.1\f[R]; default: \f[B]both\f[R])
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--range-format\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]long\f[R]|\f[B]short\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Accept long/short range formats (default: \f[B]both\f[R]) -
\f[B]long\f[R]: \f[V]192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\f[R] - \f[B]short\f[R]:
\f[V]192.0.2.0-255\f[R]
.SS Common Use Cases
.PP
\f[B]Enforce CIDR-only patterns\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Only allow CIDR in search patterns; accept any format in data
grepnet --parse-cidr both --parse-netmask input \[rs]
  --parse-hostmask input --parse-ranges input \[rs]
  --parse-bare-ip input \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Strict format validation\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Accept only CIDR everywhere; reject all other formats
grepnet --parse-cidr both --parse-netmask none \[rs]
  --parse-hostmask none --parse-ranges none \[rs]
  --parse-bare-ip none \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Accept flexible input, output CIDR only\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract networks in any format from input, output as standardized CIDR
grepnet --extract --format cidr data.txt
# (No parsing flags needed; extraction normalizes everything to CIDR)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Bare IP only (no CIDR interpretation)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Find exact IPs only; don\[aq]t interpret as /32 networks
grepnet --parse-bare-ip both --parse-cidr none \[rs]
  --parse-netmask none --parse-hostmask none \[rs]
  --parse-ranges none 192.0.2.1 data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Disable potentially ambiguous formats\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Turn off bare IPs (often ambiguous in mixed data) and hostmask (rarely used)
grepnet --parse-bare-ip none --parse-hostmask none \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Asymmetric Parsing Example
.PP
\f[B]Scenario\f[R]: Accept subnet masks in configuration files (old
format), but patterns must use CIDR (modern standard).
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --parse-cidr both \[rs]
        --parse-netmask input \[rs]
        --parse-ranges none \[rs]
        --parse-bare-ip none \[rs]
        \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Result\f[R]: - Pattern \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R] accepted ✓ (CIDR in
pattern) - File line \f[V]server 192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0\f[R] matches ✓
(netmask in input) - File line \f[V]range 192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\f[R]
does NOT match ✗ (ranges disabled)
.SH PREFIX LENGTH FILTERING
.PP
Prefix length (also called CIDR mask size or network mask bits)
determines how many IP addresses a network block contains.
Lower numbers = larger networks, higher numbers = smaller networks.
.PP
\f[B]Prefix length basics\f[R]: - IPv4: \f[V]/0\f[R] to \f[V]/32\f[R] (0
= entire internet, 32 = single IP) - IPv6: \f[V]/0\f[R] to
\f[V]/128\f[R] (0 = entire IPv6 space, 128 = single IP) - Each increment
halves the network size: \f[V]/24\f[R] has 256 IPs, \f[V]/25\f[R] has
128 IPs
.PP
\f[B]Why filter by prefix length\f[R]: - \f[B]Audit subnetting\f[R]:
Find networks larger/smaller than policy allows - \f[B]Simplify
output\f[R]: Extract only top-level aggregates or leaf subnets -
\f[B]Validate consistency\f[R]: Ensure all networks are within expected
size range - \f[B]Find anomalies\f[R]: Detect over-aggressive subnetting
or under-utilized aggregates
.PP
\f[B]Terminology clarification\f[R]: - \f[B]Shorter\f[R] prefix = fewer
bits = LARGER network (more IPs) - \f[V]/8\f[R] is \[lq]shorter\[rq]
than \f[V]/24\f[R] (contains 65,536x more IPs) - \f[B]Longer\f[R] prefix
= more bits = SMALLER network (fewer IPs) - \f[V]/24\f[R] is
\[lq]longer\[rq] than \f[V]/8\f[R] (contains fewer IPs)
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]\[en]ipv4-shorter-than\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Include only IPv4 networks with prefix length < N (larger networks)
.TP
\f[B]\[en]ipv4-longer-than\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Include only IPv4 networks with prefix length > N (smaller networks)
.TP
\f[B]\[en]ipv6-shorter-than\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Include only IPv6 networks with prefix length < N (larger networks)
.TP
\f[B]\[en]ipv6-longer-than\f[R] \f[I]N\f[R]
Include only IPv6 networks with prefix length > N (smaller networks)
.SS Common Use Cases
.PP
\f[B]Find small subnets (more specific)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Only /25 or smaller (more granular subdivisions)
grepnet --extract --ipv4-longer-than=24 networks.txt

# Result: /25, /26, /27, ... /32 only
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Find large aggregates (less specific)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Only /16 or larger (broad coverage)
grepnet --extract --ipv4-shorter-than=16 networks.txt

# Result: /0, /1, /2, ... /15 only
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Find networks in a specific size range\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Only class C or smaller (between /24 and /32)
grepnet --extract --ipv4-longer-than=24 --ipv4-shorter-than=32 networks.txt

# Result: /25, /26, /27, /28, /29, /30, /31 only
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Audit IPv6 allocation (common /64 boundary)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Find subnets smaller than standard /64 allocation
grepnet --extract --ipv6-longer-than=64 networks.txt

# Result: /65, /66, ... /128 (unusual, needs review)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Find policy violations\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Policy: allocate in /25 blocks minimum
# Find anything smaller (over-subnetting)
grepnet --extract --ipv4-longer-than=25 violations.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Consolidation validation\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Consolidate, then show only large aggregates (verify reduction)
grepnet --consolidate --ipv4-shorter-than=20 all_networks.txt

# Result: Only /0 to /19 (summary-level networks)
\f[R]
.fi
.SH STRICT CIDR VALIDATION
.PP
CIDR validation ensures that network addresses don\[cq]t have \[lq]host
bits\[rq] set.
For example, \f[V]192.0.2.1/24\f[R] is technically invalid (host bits
are set); the correct form is \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Why strict validation matters\f[R]: - \f[B]Consistency\f[R]:
Ensures all networks are represented canonically - \f[B]Debugging\f[R]:
Catches typos (e.g., pattern meant to be \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R] but
written as \f[V]192.0.2.1/24\f[R]) - \f[B]Compliance\f[R]: Some network
configurations use CIDR notation to denote both the interface IP address
and the network in one string (e.g., \f[V]192.0.2.1/24\f[R] means
\[lq]configure IP 192.0.2.1 on the 192.0.2.0/24 network\[rq]).
Strict CIDR validation distinguishes between these uses.
- \f[B]Safety\f[R]: Avoid ambiguous network definitions
.PP
\f[B]Default behavior\f[R]: Non-strict (normalize silently).
Malformed CIDRs like \f[V]192.0.2.1/24\f[R] are automatically corrected
to \f[V]192.0.2.0/24\f[R] without warning.
This provides flexibility but may hide mistakes.
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--strict-cidr\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]pattern\f[R]|\f[B]input\f[R]|\f[B]both\f[R]|\f[B]none\f[R]}
Where to apply strict CIDR validation (default: \f[B]none\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]pattern\f[R]: Validate search patterns only (reject malformed
patterns)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]input\f[R]: Validate data file content only (reject malformed
networks found in files)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]both\f[R]: Validate both patterns and input
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]none\f[R]: No strict validation (default; silently normalize)
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--strict-cidr-action\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]fail\f[R]|\f[B]normalize\f[R]|\f[B]ignore\f[R]|\f[B]warn\f[R]}
Action when invalid CIDR detected (default: \f[B]normalize\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]fail\f[R] - Exit with error code 2 (stop processing)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]normalize\f[R] - Silently correct to network address (default)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]ignore\f[R] - Skip invalid CIDR (don\[cq]t include in results)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]warn\f[R] - Print warning to stderr, then skip
.SS Use Cases
.PP
\f[B]Audit configuration consistency\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Reject any malformed CIDR in input; show warnings
grepnet --strict-cidr input --strict-cidr-action warn \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Enforce pattern validation\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Reject if user types malformed pattern (e.g., typo in script)
grepnet --strict-cidr pattern --strict-cidr-action fail \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Strict everywhere with warnings\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Both patterns and input must be valid; warn on violations
grepnet --strict-cidr both --strict-cidr-action warn \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Silent normalization (default)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Accept \[ga]192.0.2.1/24\[ga], automatically correct to \[ga]192.0.2.0/24\[ga]
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
# No warnings; output treats both as \[ga]192.0.2.0/24\[ga]
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Visual Highlighting of Normalized CIDRs
.PP
When a CIDR has been normalized (host bits were corrected), grepnet can
highlight it in output using the \f[V]GREPNET_COLORS\f[R] environment
variable.
The color key \f[V]mn\f[R] controls the appearance of normalized CIDRs.
.PP
\f[B]Default\f[R]: Normalized CIDRs are underlined (\f[V]04\f[R])
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]: Highlight normalized CIDRs in red:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mn=01;31\[dq]
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
# Any network found as 192.0.2.1/24 (normalized to 192.0.2.0/24) will be displayed in red
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
This helps visually identify which networks required correction, making
it easy to spot potential data quality issues without enabling strict
mode errors.
.PP
\f[B]Color code format\f[R]: ANSI escape codes (e.g., \f[V]01;31\f[R] =
bold red) - \f[V]01;31\f[R] - Bold red - \f[V]01;33\f[R] - Bold yellow -
\f[V]01;36\f[R] - Bold cyan - \f[V]04\f[R] - Underline - \f[V]0\f[R] -
Default color
.SS Strict Validation with Extraction and Consolidation
.PP
Strict CIDR validation is especially useful with \f[V]--extract\f[R] and
\f[V]--consolidate\f[R] modes, since these operations normalize all
networks to canonical form and the distinction between correct and
malformed input becomes significant.
.PP
\f[B]Extraction behavior\f[R]: - Non-strict (default): All networks
extracted and normalized, no warning - Strict mode: Rejects/warns/skips
networks with host bits set (depending on action) - Use case: Audit data
quality before consolidation
.PP
\f[B]Consolidation behavior\f[R]: - Strict validation applies during
extraction phase (before merging) - Consolidation result: Only valid
networks are merged - Use case: Ensure consolidated output contains only
canonical networks
.PP
\f[B]Example: Audit and clean network data\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Strict input validation with warnings, then consolidate
grepnet --extract --strict-cidr input --strict-cidr-action warn \[rs]
  --consolidate --format cidr config.txt

# Output: Only canonical networks, with warnings for any malformed input
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Example: Identify data quality issues before consolidation\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Show which networks needed normalization
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mn=01;31\[dq]
grepnet --extract --format cidr config.txt

# Red-highlighted networks indicate data with host bits set
# Use --strict-cidr input --strict-cidr-action fail to reject these
\f[R]
.fi
.SH BINARY FILE HANDLING
.TP
\f[B]-a\f[R], \f[V]--text\f[R]
Treat all files as text
.TP
\f[B]-I\f[R]
Skip binary files
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--binary-files\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]binary\f[R]|\f[B]text\f[R]|\f[B]without-match\f[R]}
How to handle binary files (default: \f[B]binary\f[R])
.SH COMMENT FILTERING
.PP
Comment filtering controls whether networks inside comments are parsed
and matched.
By default, comments are stripped during network parsing to prevent
false matches from commented-out configurations or documentation.
.PP
\f[B]What gets filtered\f[R]: - \f[B]Pattern parsing\f[R]: Comments in
pattern arguments (e.g.,
\f[V]grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24 # my network\[aq] file.txt\f[R] → comment
stripped from pattern) - \f[B]File content parsing\f[R]: Comments in
searched files (e.g., \f[V]allow 192.0.2.0/24 # production\f[R] →
comment stripped before matching)
.PP
\f[B]How it works\f[R]: 1.
\f[B]Pattern parsing phase\f[R]: Pattern arguments parsed; comments
stripped; networks extracted from non-comment portion 2.
\f[B]File parsing phase\f[R]: File lines parsed; comments stripped;
networks extracted from non-comment portion
.PD 0
.P
.PD
3.
\f[B]Matching phase\f[R]: Extracted networks from patterns matched
against extracted networks from files 4.
\f[B]Output phase\f[R]: The original file line WITH comments is printed
(comments appear uncolorized; see COLOR OUTPUT section)
.PP
\f[B]Recognized comment markers\f[R]: \f[V]#\f[R], \f[V]!\f[R],
\f[V]--\f[R], \f[V]//\f[R], \f[V]/* */\f[R], \f[V];\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]Critical distinction\f[R]: The entire line is output when matched,
but networks in the comment portion are NOT parsed, extracted, or
counted in results.
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--no-filter-comments\f[B]\f[R]
Disable comment filtering; parse and match networks even if they appear
in comments (applies to both patterns and file content)
.SS Realistic Examples
.PP
\f[B]Configuration file with commented defaults\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
Input file:
allow 192.0.2.0/24         # active rule
# allow 192.0.3.0/24       # commented rule

grepnet \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] config.txt

Default (comments filtered):
allow [highlighted]192.0.2.1[reset]          # active rule
(commented line skipped entirely)

With --no-filter-comments:
allow [highlighted]192.0.2.0/24[reset]       # active rule
# allow [highlighted]192.0.3.0/24[reset]     # commented rule
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Why this matters for matching\f[R]: - \f[B]Default\f[R]: Only
active configurations are matched (commented rules are ignored) -
\f[V]--no-filter-comments\f[R]: Both active and commented rules are
matched (useful for auditing all references)
.SS Impact on \[en]extract and \[en]consolidate
.PP
Comment filtering significantly affects data extraction and
consolidation:
.PP
\f[B]Default behavior (comments filtered)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract network.conf

Input file:
192.0.2.0/25
# 192.0.2.128/25
192.0.2.0/24

Output:
192.0.2.0/25
192.0.2.0/24
(commented 192.0.2.128/25 is excluded)

Then consolidate:
192.0.2.0/24 (result: 192.0.2.0/25 + 192.0.2.0/24 merge)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]With \[en]no-filter-comments\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --no-filter-comments network.conf

Same input, but commented network IS extracted:
192.0.2.0/25
192.0.2.128/25
192.0.2.0/24

Then consolidate:
192.0.2.0/24 (result: all three merge into single /24)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Impact on network inventory\f[R]: - \f[B]Default\f[R]: Your network
inventory excludes commented configurations (cleaner, reflects
\[lq]active\[rq] state) - \f[V]--no-filter-comments\f[R]: Your inventory
includes all configurations (useful for compliance audits or finding
forgotten commented entries)
.SS Common Use Cases
.PP
\f[B]Normal configuration auditing\f[R] (comments filtered by default):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract active networks from config files
grepnet --extract --consolidate firewall.conf

# Result: Only networks in active (non-commented) lines
# Commented networks like \[dq]# old: 192.0.2.0/25\[dq] are excluded
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Full inventory audit\f[R] (including commented configurations):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract ALL networks including commented ones
grepnet --extract --no-filter-comments --consolidate firewall.conf

# Result: Comprehensive inventory including \[dq]dormant\[dq] commented networks
# Helps find forgotten commented-out entries
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Verifying consolidation impact\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Show difference between filtered and unfiltered
echo \[dq]=== Active only ===\[dq]
grepnet --extract --consolidate --format cidr config.txt

echo \[dq]=== All including commented ===\[dq]
grepnet --extract --no-filter-comments --consolidate --format cidr config.txt

# Difference highlights networks only in comments
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Documentation extraction\f[R] (with commented examples):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract code examples from documentation that show network usage
grepnet --extract --no-filter-comments *.md *.rst

# Captures both networks in prose and in code/comments
\f[R]
.fi
.SH COLOR OUTPUT
.PP
Colored output helps identify different parts of grepnet results at a
glance.
Each element (filename, line number, matched networks, match types,
etc.)
can have its own color.
.PP
\f[B]Output structure with colors\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
[filename]:[line_number]:[matched_line_content]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Different elements can be styled independently: - \f[B]filename\f[R]:
File containing the match - \f[B]line_number\f[R]: Line number of the
match - \f[B]match_type colors\f[R]: Different colors for
equals/subset/superset/overlap/unmatched relationships -
\f[B]normalized_marker\f[R]: Highlight CIDRs that required normalization
(host bits stripped) - \f[B]context_lines\f[R]: Different style for
context (non-matching) lines
.SS Flags
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--color\f[B]\f[R] {\f[B]auto\f[R]|\f[B]always\f[R]|\f[B]never\f[R]}
When to use colored output (default: \f[B]auto\f[R])
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]auto\f[R]: Colors on terminal output, plain text when output is
piped or redirected
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]always\f[R]: Force colored output even when piped (useful for
terminal multiplexers, log files)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]never\f[R]: Disable colors (useful for scripts, CI/CD pipelines)
.PP
\f[B]Example - Force colors even when piped\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --color always \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.txt | less -R
# -R tells less to interpret ANSI color codes
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Customizing Colors with GREPNET_COLORS
.PP
Use the \f[V]GREPNET_COLORS\f[R] environment variable to customize any
color element.
Format: space-separated KEY=ANSI_CODE pairs.
.PP
\f[B]Supported color keys\f[R]:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Key
T}@T{
Meaning
T}@T{
Default
T}
_
T{
\f[V]fn\f[R]
T}@T{
Filename
T}@T{
35 (magenta)
T}
T{
\f[V]ln\f[R]
T}@T{
Line number
T}@T{
32 (green)
T}
T{
\f[V]bn\f[R]
T}@T{
Byte offset
T}@T{
32 (green)
T}
T{
\f[V]se\f[R]
T}@T{
Separator (: or
T}@T{
)
T}
T{
\f[V]sl\f[R]
T}@T{
Selected line background
T}@T{
0 (none)
T}
T{
\f[V]cx\f[R]
T}@T{
Context line styling
T}@T{
0 (none)
T}
T{
\f[V]mt\f[R]
T}@T{
Match text
T}@T{
0 (none)
T}
T{
\f[V]ms\f[R]
T}@T{
Match separator within line
T}@T{
0 (none)
T}
T{
\f[V]mc\f[R]
T}@T{
Match colon
T}@T{
0 (none)
T}
T{
\f[V]me\f[R]
T}@T{
EQUALS match type
T}@T{
01;32 (bright green)
T}
T{
\f[V]mb\f[R]
T}@T{
SUBSET match type
T}@T{
01;34 (bright blue)
T}
T{
\f[V]mS\f[R]
T}@T{
SUPERSET match type
T}@T{
01;33 (bright yellow)
T}
T{
\f[V]mo\f[R]
T}@T{
OVERLAP match type
T}@T{
01;31 (bright red)
T}
T{
\f[V]mu\f[R]
T}@T{
UNMATCHED networks
T}@T{
0 (none)
T}
T{
\f[V]mn\f[R]
T}@T{
Normalized CIDR (host bits fixed)
T}@T{
04 (underline)
T}
.TE
.PP
\f[B]ANSI color codes\f[R] (SGR - Select Graphic Rendition):
.PP
Basic codes: - \f[V]0\f[R] = Reset to default - \f[V]01\f[R] = Bold -
\f[V]04\f[R] = Underline - \f[V]07\f[R] = Reverse video (inverse)
.PP
Foreground colors: - \f[V]31\f[R] = Red - \f[V]32\f[R] = Green -
\f[V]33\f[R] = Yellow - \f[V]34\f[R] = Blue - \f[V]35\f[R] = Magenta -
\f[V]36\f[R] = Cyan - \f[V]37\f[R] = White
.PP
\f[B]Combining attributes\f[R] (stackable ANSI codes): ANSI codes can be
combined with semicolons to stack multiple attributes.
Each key can have multiple codes separated by semicolons: - Combine
\f[V]01\f[R] (bold) + color: \f[V]01;31\f[R] = bold red - Combine
\f[V]04\f[R] (underline) + color: \f[V]04;32\f[R] = underlined green
.PD 0
.P
.PD
- Combine \f[V]07\f[R] (reverse video) + color: \f[V]07;33\f[R] =
reverse yellow - Combine multiple attributes: \f[V]01;04;35\f[R] = bold
underlined magenta
.PP
\f[B]Stackable attributes\f[R] (can be combined with color codes): -
\f[V]01\f[R] = Bold (makes colors brighter/more intense) - \f[V]04\f[R]
= Underline (adds underline to text) - \f[V]07\f[R] = Reverse video
(swaps foreground and background)
.PP
\f[B]Stackable color keys\f[R] (which keys can have attributes
combined): Most color keys support combining bold/underline/reverse with
a foreground color: - \f[V]me\f[R], \f[V]mb\f[R], \f[V]mS\f[R],
\f[V]mo\f[R] (match types): Can stack bold, underline, reverse with
colors - \f[V]mn\f[R] (normalized CIDR): Can stack bold, underline,
reverse with colors - \f[V]fn\f[R], \f[V]ln\f[R], \f[V]bn\f[R],
\f[V]se\f[R], \f[V]mt\f[R], \f[V]ms\f[R], \f[V]mc\f[R] (structural
elements): Can stack attributes with colors - \f[V]mu\f[R] (unmatched):
Can stack attributes with colors - \f[V]sl\f[R], \f[V]cx\f[R] (styling):
Can use standalone attributes or with colors
.PP
\f[B]Example stackable keys\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# mn (normalized CIDR) with bold red
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mn=01;31\[dq]

# mn with underline and bold green
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mn=01;04;32\[dq]

# Subset match type (mb) with reverse cyan
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mb=07;36\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Not stackable\f[R] (are mutually exclusive - use only one per key):
- Foreground colors (\f[V]31-37\f[R]) - pick one color per key -
Background colors (\f[V]41-47\f[R]) - if used, pick one background color
.SS Common Customizations
.PP
\f[B]Make all matches bright red\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]me=01;31 mb=01;31 mS=01;31 mo=01;31\[dq]
grepnet \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] *.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Color by match type (standard but more saturated)\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]me=01;32 mb=01;36 mS=01;33 mo=01;31\[dq]
# Green for equals, cyan for subset, yellow for superset, red for overlap
grepnet \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] *.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Highlight normalized CIDRs for data quality audit\f[R] (mn is
stackable):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mn=01;31\[dq]
# Normalized CIDRs (those with host bits stripped) shown in bold red
grepnet --no-filter-comments \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Highlight normalized CIDRs with underline instead\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]mn=04;33\[dq]
# Normalized CIDRs shown in underlined yellow (easier for some viewers)
grepnet --no-filter-comments \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]High contrast for accessibility\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]fn=01;37 ln=01;32 me=01;36 mb=01;36 mS=01;33 mo=01;31\[dq]
# Filenames: bold white
# Line numbers: bold green
# Matches: cyan, cyan, yellow, red
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Minimal/no highlighting\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]me=0 mb=0 mS=0 mo=0 mn=0\[dq]
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.SS Color Codes in Output
.PP
When colored output is enabled, ANSI escape sequences are embedded in
the output.
These look like \f[V][ESC[...m\f[R] in raw form but render as colors in
terminal emulators.
.PP
\f[B]Example raw output\f[R] (what\[cq]s in the terminal):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
\[rs]033[35mfile.txt\[rs]033[36m:\[rs]033[32m42\[rs]033[36m:\[rs]033[01;32m192.0.2.0/24\[rs]033[0m other text
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Renders as\f[R] (what you see):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
file.txt:42:[highlighted]192.0.2.0/24[reset] other text
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Piping with colors\f[R]: - Default (\f[V]--color auto\f[R]): Colors
are removed when output is piped - With \f[V]--color always\f[R]: Colors
are preserved (useful for \f[V]less -R\f[R], \f[V]more\f[R], etc.)
- For scripts: Use \f[V]--color never\f[R] to avoid color codes in
captured output
.SH DATA FORMATS
.TP
\f[B]-z\f[R], \f[V]--null-data\f[R]
Treat input/output as NUL-terminated records instead of newlines
.TP
\f[B]-Z\f[R], \f[V]--null\f[R]
Output NUL after filename instead of colon
.PP
Useful for safe handling of filenames with special characters:
\f[V]grepnet -lZ \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] *.txt | xargs -0 cat\f[R]
.SH DEBUGGING
.SH DEBUGGING
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--debug\f[B]\f[R] [\f[I]FUNCTIONS\f[R]] \f[B](DEPRECATED)\f[R]
Enable debug output to stderr
.PP
Produces verbose output useful for troubleshooting pattern matching,
network extraction, and filtering logic.
Debug output goes to stderr to avoid interfering with normal stdout
results.
.PP
\f[B]⚠ Deprecation notice\f[R]: This flag is deprecated and may be
removed in a future version.
Use stderr redirection or external debugging tools instead.
.SH MESSAGES AND INFORMATION
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--no-messages\f[B]\f[R]
Suppress error and warning messages
.PP
Useful in scripts or automated workflows where you want to rely on exit
status codes without stderr output.
.TP
\f[B]\f[VB]--version\f[B]\f[R]
Show version and exit
.PP
Displays grepnet version number and exits.
.TP
\f[B]-h\f[R], \f[V]--help\f[R]
Show help and exit
.PP
Displays command-line help and exits.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Matches found
.TP
1
No matches found, or no matching files found (with \f[V]-l\f[R] /
\f[V]-L\f[R])
.TP
2
Error occurred (invalid arguments, file not found, pattern error, etc.)
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Searching
.PP
\f[B]Find all references to a network in a file:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Searches for all lines in config.txt containing any IP address within
the 192.0.2.0/24 network.
.PP
\f[B]Match a single IP against a CIDR pattern:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
echo \[dq]192.0.2.50\[dq] | grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Accepts input from stdin; tests if 192.0.2.50 is within the pattern
network.
.PP
\f[B]Search multiple files:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] server1.txt server2.txt server3.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Searches all three files for any IP in the 10.0.0.0/8 range, with output
grouped by file.
.PP
\f[B]Search entire directory tree:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] /etc/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Recursively searches all files under /etc/ for networks in the
192.0.0.0/16 block.
.SS Multiple Patterns
.PP
\f[B]Using a single pattern is most common:\f[R]
.PP
For most use cases, you only need one pattern.
The pattern can be any format:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# CIDR notation
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt

# IP range
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0-192.0.2.255\[aq] data.txt

# Subnet mask
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0 255.255.255.0\[aq] data.txt

# Single IP
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.1\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]When you need multiple patterns, use \f[VB]-P\f[B] or
\f[VB]-f\f[B]:\f[R]
.PP
Search for lines matching ANY of multiple patterns (logical OR):
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -P \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] -P \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Each pattern can be in a different format:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -P \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] -P \[aq]10.0.0.0-10.255.255.255\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
\f[B]Read multiple patterns from file:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -f allowed_networks.txt config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Reads patterns from allowed_networks.txt (one per line).
Each line is a separate pattern in any supported format:
.PP
\f[B]Example allowed_networks.txt:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
192.0.2.0/24
203.0.113.0 255.255.255.0
198.51.100.0-198.51.100.255
10.0.0.0/8
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
All four patterns are valid; each can be a different format.
The file has 4 patterns total, not 4 copies of the same pattern.
.SS Match Type Filtering
.PP
\f[B]Find only exact matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -e \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Returns only lines containing exactly 192.0.2.0/24 (no 192.0.2.0/25, no
192.0.2.1, no 192.0.0.0/16).
.PP
\f[B]Find networks contained within the pattern:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -s \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] all_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Finds all subnets that fit inside 192.0.0.0/16: returns 192.0.2.0/24,
192.0.3.0/24, etc.
Useful for policy audits: \[lq]which smaller networks have been
allocated from this block?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Find networks that contain the pattern:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -S \[aq]192.0.2.1\[aq] subnets.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Finds all networks where 192.0.2.1 is a member.
If subnets.txt contains 192.0.0.0/16, 192.0.2.0/24, 192.0.2.128/25, all
three match.
Useful for traceability: \[lq]which networks claim this IP?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Show all matches with their match types:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -P \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] -P \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Uses grepnet\[cq]s default match-type coloring (if colors enabled) to
show EQUALS (⊆⊇), SUBSET (⊆), SUPERSET (⊇), OVERLAP, or UNMATCHED.
.SS Output Formatting
.PP
\f[B]Show line numbers:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -n \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Prepends line numbers to output.
Useful for referencing specific matches in large config files.
.PP
\f[B]Show only the matched networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -o \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Extracts just the matching network/IP strings, one per line.
Removes line numbers and context.
Useful for creating lists of found networks for further processing.
.PP
\f[B]Count matching lines:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -c \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Outputs count of matching lines per file.
Use when you need statistics: \[lq]how many rules reference this
subnet?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Show only filenames with matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -l \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] *.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Lists only files containing matches (without the actual lines).
Efficient for large file sets.
Useful in scripts:
\f[V]grepnet -l pattern files | xargs process_files\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Count total matches across all files:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -c \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.txt | awk -F: \[aq]{sum+=$2} END {print sum}\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Combines -c with awk to get total matches across multiple files.
.SS Context Display
.PP
\f[B]Show 2 lines of context around matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -C 2 \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Displays the matched line plus 2 lines before and 2 lines after.
Useful for understanding configuration context (e.g., seeing which
rule/policy a network belongs to).
.PP
\f[B]Show 1 line before, 3 lines after:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -B 1 -A 3 \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] audit.log
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Asymmetric context: 1 line before (often the timestamp/event header) and
3 lines after (related details).
Efficient for log analysis.
.PP
\f[B]Context without group separator:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -C 1 --no-group-separator \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] file.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Displays context continuously without \f[V]--\f[R] separators between
groups.
Cleaner output for piping or log analysis.
.PP
\f[B]Display context with line numbers and colored matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -nC 2 --color always \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] audit.log | head -50
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Combines line numbers, context, and colors for visual identification in
large logs.
.SS Network Extraction
.PP
\f[B]Extract all unique networks from file:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract firewall_rules.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Scans the file, finds all network objects (CIDR blocks, ranges, bare
IPs), and outputs each unique network once.
Useful for inventory: \[lq]what networks are referenced in this
config?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Extract and normalize to CIDR:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --format cidr firewall_rules.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Converts all formats (netmask notation, ranges, bare IPs) to CIDR.
Output is color-coded (if colors enabled): normalized CIDRs are
underlined by default.
.PP
\f[B]Extract networks matching a pattern:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract -P \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] all_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Extracts only networks that are subsets of 192.0.0.0/16.
Useful for extracting allocated subnets from a larger block.
.PP
\f[B]Consolidate overlapping networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --consolidate all_subnets.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Merges overlapping/adjacent networks into minimal set.
Input might have 192.0.2.0/24 and 192.0.3.0/24 as separate entries;
output combines into 192.0.2.0/23 (if adjacent).
.PP
\f[B]Output consolidated networks as ranges:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --consolidate --format range networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Shows consolidated result in IP range format (e.g.,
192.0.2.0-192.0.3.255) instead of CIDR.
Useful for some firewall/ACL formats.
.PP
\f[B]Extract and show source lines:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --show-content config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Outputs both extracted networks AND the original config lines they came
from.
Useful for traceability: \[lq]this network is from this config
line.\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Extract, consolidate, and save as new config:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --consolidate --format cidr firewall_rules.txt > consolidated.txt
grepnet -f consolidated.txt application.conf  # now search using consolidated rules
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Pipeline: extract all networks from rules, consolidate them, then use
that consolidated set to search application configs.
.SS Exclusion
.PP
\f[B]Exclude deprecated networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-string \[aq]deprecated\[aq] \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Finds all IPs in 192.0.0.0/16, but skips any line containing the word
\[lq]deprecated\[rq].
Useful for active inventory vs.\ archived/commented configs.
.PP
\f[B]Exclude specific network from results:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.2.0/25\[aq] \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Finds all IPs in 192.0.2.0/24, but excludes the 192.0.2.0/25 subset (and
anything smaller within it).
Returns only 192.0.2.128/25 block results.
.PP
\f[B]Exclude networks from file:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidrs-file banned.txt \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Reads exclusion list from banned.txt (one network per line) and excludes
all of them from search results.
Useful for blocklist-style filtering: \[lq]show me everything except
these networks.\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Exclude internal networks from search results:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] --exclude-cidr \[aq]172.16.0.0/12\[aq] \[rs]
  --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.168.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] firewall.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Searches entire IP space but excludes RFC 1918 private ranges.
Returns only public internet routes.
.SS Exclusion with Strict Behavior
.PP
\f[B]Default subset: include exact matches and containment:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Excludes 192.0.0.0/16 (exact match) AND 192.0.2.0/24 (subset).
This is the default: if pattern is subset of exclusion, it\[cq]s
removed.
.PP
\f[B]Strict subset: exclude only exact matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidr-behaviour subset-strict --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Excludes only 192.0.0.0/16 (exact match).
Does NOT exclude 192.0.2.0/24 (subset of exclusion), returns 10.0.0.0/8
and 192.0.2.0/24.
Useful: \[lq]I only want to exclude this specific network, not
everything within it.\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Default superset: include exact matches and containment:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidr-behaviour superset --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Excludes 192.0.2.0/24 (exact match) AND 192.0.0.0/16 (superset that
contains it).
Filters based on \[lq]anything that includes this network.\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Strict superset: exclude only exact matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --exclude-cidr-behaviour superset-strict --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Excludes only 192.0.2.0/24 (exact match).
Does NOT exclude 192.0.0.0/16 (superset).
Useful: \[lq]I want to exclude this specific allocation, but keep the
larger blocks.\[rq]
.SS Recursive Searching
.PP
\f[B]Find all references in configuration directory:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] /etc/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Searches all files under /etc/ recursively, printing matches with
file:line format.
Output is grouped by file.
.PP
\f[B]Include only .conf and .yaml files:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r --include \[aq]*.conf\[aq] --include \[aq]*.yaml\[aq] \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] /etc/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Filters to only .conf and .yaml files; binary and text-only files are
skipped.
Efficient when you know the file types.
.PP
\f[B]Exclude backup directories:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r --exclude-dir \[aq]backup\[aq] --exclude-dir \[aq]archive\[aq] \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] /var/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Skips entire backup and archive directory trees.
More efficient than \f[V]--exclude\f[R] patterns because directory
contents are not even traversed.
.PP
\f[B]Search logs, exclude archived subdirectories:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r --exclude \[aq]*/archive/*\[aq] \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] /var/log/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Pattern-based exclusion: any path matching \f[V]*/archive/*\f[R] is
skipped.
Useful for excluding specific nested patterns.
.PP
\f[B]Find files containing matches:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -rl \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] /etc/ | while read f; do echo \[dq]=== $f ===\[dq]; head -5 \[dq]$f\[dq]; done
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Lists files, then displays first 5 lines of each.
Quick overview of which configs reference a network.
.SS Binary File Handling
.PP
\f[B]Process binary files as text:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -a \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] data.bin
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Forces binary files to be treated as text.
Useful for config files that might have binary encoding or compiled
configs (e.g., SQLite, protocol buffers).
.PP
\f[B]Report binary matches without displaying content:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --binary-files without-match \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] *.bin
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
When binary file contains a match, report \[lq]match found\[rq] but
don\[cq]t output the binary garbage.
Useful for counting/logging without corrupting terminal.
.PP
\f[B]Safely search mixed binary/text directory:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r --binary-files skip \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] ./
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Skips binary files entirely, searches only text files.
Fastest approach when you know what you\[cq]re looking for.
.SS Format Options
.PP
\f[B]Search using netmask notation in patterns:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --parse-pattern netmask \[aq]192.0.2.0/255.255.255.0\[aq] data.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Allows patterns to use netmask format instead of only CIDR.
Matches against any format in input file.
.PP
\f[B]Accept only CIDR format in input files:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --parse-input cidr \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Enforces CIDR notation in input file; rejects netmask/range format.
Useful for strict validation: \[lq]this file must use only CIDR.\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Extract and output as host/mask pairs:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --format host-mask networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Output shows base IP and separate netmask (e.g., 192.0.2.0
255.255.255.0) instead of CIDR notation.
.SS Prefix Length Filtering
.PP
\f[B]Find subnets smaller than /24 (more specific):\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --ipv4-longer-than=24 networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Returns all IPv4 networks with prefix longer than /24 (e.g., /25, /26,
/27).
Useful for finding over-allocation: \[lq]where are we subdividing too
much?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Find networks larger than /16 (less specific):\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --ipv4-shorter-than=16 networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Returns all IPv4 networks with prefix shorter than /16 (e.g., /8, /12,
/15).
Useful for finding aggregates: \[lq]which large blocks do we own?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Find exact prefix length:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --ipv4-prefix-length=24 networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Returns only /24 networks.
Useful for compliance checking: \[lq]do we only allocate /24 to branch
offices?\[rq] or \[lq]which networks don\[cq]t follow our standard?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Find specific range of prefix lengths:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --ipv4-min-prefix-length=20 --ipv4-max-prefix-length=24 networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Returns networks between /20 and /24.
Useful for policy validation: \[lq]all allocations must be between /20
and /24.\[rq]
.SS Parsing and Comments
.PP
\f[B]Search file with commented networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --no-filter-comments \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] config.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Enables parsing of networks in comments.
By default, commented networks are ignored; this includes them in the
search.
.PP
\f[B]Show which networks are active vs commented:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Count active networks only (default)
grepnet --extract config.txt | wc -l

# Count all networks including commented ones
grepnet --no-filter-comments --extract config.txt | wc -l
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Useful for inventory: how many networks are actually active vs.\ how
many are defined but commented out?
.PP
\f[B]Extract only from non-commented lines:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract config.txt > active_networks.txt
# Then search using only active networks
grepnet -f active_networks.txt firewall.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Two-step pipeline: extract active networks, then use that whitelist.
.SS Advanced Combinations
.PP
\f[B]Whitelist-based filtering:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract our authorized networks
grepnet --extract authorized.conf > our_networks.txt

# Find all mentions of our networks in logs
grepnet -f our_networks.txt /var/log/firewall.log

# Exclude any hits on internal networks
grepnet -f our_networks.txt --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] /var/log/firewall.log
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Multi-step pipeline: define authorized networks, then search and filter
strategically.
.PP
\f[B]Blacklist-based filtering:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract blocked networks
grepnet --extract banned_networks.txt > blocklist.txt

# Search logs, but exclude anything matching the blocklist
grepnet \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] /var/log/*.log --exclude-cidrs-file blocklist.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Inverted approach: search everything, then remove known/expected
matches.
.PP
\f[B]Audit network allocation:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --consolidate corporate_allocation.txt > current_state.txt
diff <(sort previous_state.txt) <(sort current_state.txt)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Track changes in network allocation: \[lq]what new networks were
added?\[rq]
.PP
\f[B]Security audit:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -rn --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] --exclude-cidr \[aq]172.16.0.0/12\[aq] \[rs]
  --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.168.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] /etc/ | grep -v \[aq]approved\[rs]|expected\[aq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Finds all external network references (excluding RFC1918), then filters
to hide approved entries.
Suspicious references stand out.
.PP
\f[B]Recursive search with context, line numbers, and colors:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -rn -C 2 --color always \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] /etc/ | less -R
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Combined flags for detailed investigation: -r (recursive), -n (line
numbers), -C 2 (2-line context), \[en]color always (force colors), piped
to \f[V]less -R\f[R] for proper color rendering.
.PP
\f[B]Find networks in logs, exclude internal, show counts per file:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -rc --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] /var/log/*.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Lists count of matches per file.
Useful for identifying which log files have the most activity for a
given network.
.PP
\f[B]Extract, normalize, and check for anomalies:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract and normalize all formats to CIDR
grepnet --extract --format cidr all_networks.txt > normalized.txt

# Highlight non-standard allocations (e.g., odd prefix lengths)
grepnet --ipv4-longer-than=25 --ipv4-shorter-than=23 normalized.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Useful for finding \[lq]weird\[rq] networks that don\[cq]t match
allocation policy.
.SH COMMON PATTERNS
.SS Security Audits
.PP
\f[B]Find all external networks referenced in system configuration:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -r --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] --exclude-cidr \[aq]172.16.0.0/12\[aq] \[rs]
  --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.168.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] /etc/
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Searches entire /etc tree for any external (non-RFC1918) network
references.
Useful for identifying unexpected internet connectivity rules or
firewall policies.
.PP
\f[B]Verify no deprecated/blocked networks are referenced:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
BLOCKED=\[dq]203.0.113.0/24 198.0.113.0/24\[dq]
for net in $BLOCKED; do
  if grepnet \[dq]$net\[dq] /etc/firewall.conf 2>/dev/null; then
    echo \[dq]SECURITY ALERT: $net found in firewall rules\[dq]
  fi
done
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Checks specific blocked networks.
Useful for ensuring deprecated ISPs or malicious networks aren\[cq]t
accidentally whitelisted.
.PP
\f[B]Find overly permissive rules:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --ipv4-shorter-than=16 firewall.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Identifies rules using large CIDR blocks (/0-/15), which may be
unnecessarily broad.
Useful for security hardening reviews.
.SS Configuration Extraction and Management
.PP
\f[B]Extract all networks from config files and consolidate:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
find /etc -name \[aq]*.conf\[aq] | xargs grepnet --extract --consolidate --format cidr > all_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Creates unified inventory of all networks across configs.
Consolidated output merges overlaps, preventing policy contradictions.
.PP
\f[B]Compare two configuration snapshots:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# First scan
grepnet --extract --consolidate config_old.txt > old_networks.txt

# After update
grepnet --extract --consolidate config_new.txt > new_networks.txt

# Compare
diff -u old_networks.txt new_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Tracks network allocation changes: added routes, removed subnets,
consolidation differences.
Useful for change control logs.
.PP
\f[B]Validate that two config files define the same networks (e.g.,
junos.conf vs cisco.conf):\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract and normalize networks from both files
grepnet --extract --format cidr junos.conf | sort > junos_networks.txt
grepnet --extract --format cidr cisco.conf | sort > cisco_networks.txt

# Compare - no output means they\[aq]re identical
diff junos_networks.txt cisco_networks.txt

# Or check the result in a script
if diff -q junos_networks.txt cisco_networks.txt >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo \[dq]SUCCESS: Both configs define the same networks\[dq]
else
  echo \[dq]FAIL: Network definitions differ\[dq]
  echo \[dq]Networks in junos but not cisco:\[dq]
  comm -23 junos_networks.txt cisco_networks.txt
  echo \[dq]Networks in cisco but not junos:\[dq]
  comm -13 junos_networks.txt cisco_networks.txt
fi
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Useful for multi-vendor configuration validation: verify that Juniper
and Cisco configs have identical network definitions, or identify
differences.
The \f[V]comm\f[R] utility shows networks unique to each file (requires
sorted input).
.PP
\f[B]Build network documentation from active config:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --format cidr --show-content firewall.conf | \[rs]
  awk \[aq]{print \[dq]- \[dq] $0}\[aq] > network_inventory.md
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Generates markdown documentation with network and source line.
Useful for policy documentation and change tracking.
.PP
\f[B]Validate configuration format consistency:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract all networks
FORMATS=$(grepnet --extract firewall.conf | grep -oE \[aq](\[ha][0-9]+\[rs].[0-9]+\[rs].[0-9]+\[rs].[0-9]+$|[0-9]+\[rs].[0-9]+\[rs].[0-9]+\[rs].[0-9]+/[0-9]+|[0-9]+-[0-9]+)\[aq])

# Check if all are CIDR
grepnet -f /dev/stdin <<< \[dq]$FORMATS\[dq] | grep -v \[aq]/\[aq] && echo \[dq]Non-CIDR formats found\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Identifies format inconsistencies, useful for standardization efforts
(e.g., \[lq]migrate all to CIDR\[rq]).
.SS Network Monitoring and Change Detection
.PP
\f[B]Check for changes to allowed networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --consolidate current_config.txt > current_state.txt
if ! diff -q previous_state.txt current_state.txt >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  echo \[dq]Network allocation changed\[dq]
  diff previous_state.txt current_state.txt
fi
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Detects any network allocation changes.
Useful for configuration monitoring/alerting systems.
.PP
\f[B]Monitor for new external network additions:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Establish baseline
grepnet -r --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] /etc/ > baseline.txt

# Check periodically for changes
grepnet -r --exclude-cidr \[aq]10.0.0.0/8\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] /etc/ > current.txt
diff baseline.txt current.txt | grep \[aq]\[ha]>\[aq] | wc -l  # count new entries
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Continuous monitoring for new external routes.
Alert threshold can be set on diff count.
.PP
\f[B]Track network references in logs:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet -c \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] /var/log/firewall.*.log | sort -t: -k2 -rn | head -10
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Shows which log files have most activity for a given network.
Useful for identifying hotspots or anomalies.
.SS Compliance and Validation
.PP
\f[B]Verify no systems reference banned networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
if grepnet --exclude-cidrs-file banned.txt \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] /etc/firewall.conf 2>/dev/null | wc -l | grep -v \[aq]\[ha]0$\[aq]; then
  echo \[dq]FAIL: Banned networks found\[dq]
  exit 1
fi
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Compliance check: ensure no blacklisted networks appear anywhere.
Useful for automated compliance tests.
.PP
\f[B]Validate allocation adheres to standards:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Policy: all production networks must be /20 to /24
if grepnet --extract --ipv4-shorter-than=20 production.conf | wc -l | grep -v \[aq]\[ha]0$\[aq]; then
  echo \[dq]FAIL: Networks too large (shorter than /20)\[dq]
  exit 1
fi
if grepnet --extract --ipv4-longer-than=24 production.conf | wc -l | grep -v \[aq]\[ha]0$\[aq]; then
  echo \[dq]FAIL: Networks too small (longer than /24)\[dq]
  exit 1
fi
echo \[dq]PASS: All networks meet allocation policy\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Strict validation: networks must fall within exactly /20-/24 range.
Useful for enforcing allocation standards.
.PP
\f[B]Audit network ownership across systems:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
TARGET=\[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq]
echo \[dq]Searching for $TARGET in all system configs...\[dq]
grepnet -r \[dq]$TARGET\[dq] /etc/ | cut -d: -f1 | sort -u | while read file; do
  OWNER=$(stat -c %U \[dq]$file\[dq] 2>/dev/null)
  echo \[dq]$file (owner: $OWNER)\[dq]
done
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Identifies which config files reference a network and their owners.
Useful for accountability and change control.
.PP
\f[B]Compare intended vs.\ actual networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Intended networks
grepnet --extract intended_config.txt | sort > intended.txt

# Actual networks in production
grepnet --extract actual_config.txt | sort > actual.txt

# Find mismatches
comm -23 actual.txt intended.txt  # networks in actual but not intended
comm -13 actual.txt intended.txt  # networks in intended but not actual
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Gap analysis: what\[cq]s deployed that wasn\[cq]t planned?
What\[cq]s planned but not deployed?
.SS Data Extraction and Processing
.PP
\f[B]Extract networks for use in other tools:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Get all internal networks, then feed to ipcalc for statistics
grepnet --extract --exclude-cidr \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] networks.conf | while read net; do
  ipcalc \[dq]$net\[dq] | grep -E \[aq]Network|Broadcast|Hosts\[aq]
done
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Combines grepnet extraction with other network tools (ipcalc, whois,
etc.).
.PP
\f[B]Create access control lists from extracted networks:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract --format host-mask allowed_networks.txt | while read ip mask; do
  echo \[dq]permit ip $ip $mask\[dq]
done > router_acl.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Converts extracted networks to router ACL format (Cisco, Juniper, etc.).
.PP
\f[B]Generate firewall rules from network list:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
grepnet --extract partner_networks.txt | sort -t. -k1,1n -k2,2n -k3,3n -k4,4n | while read net; do
  echo \[dq]-A PARTNER_NETS -d $net -j ACCEPT\[dq]
done > partner_rules.conf
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Generates iptables rules from network inventory.
Sorted output creates optimized rule ordering.
.PP
\f[B]Find gaps in IP space allocation:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Extract all allocated subnets
grepnet --extract allocated.txt | sort -t/ -k2 -rn > sorted_subnets.txt

# Use ipcalc to find available space
# (script would compute gaps between allocations)
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Identifies available IP space for new allocations.
.SS Troubleshooting and Debugging
.PP
\f[B]Trace where a specific network appears:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
TARGET=\[aq]192.0.2.128/25\[aq]
echo \[dq]Searching for $TARGET in all configs...\[dq]
grepnet -rn \[dq]$TARGET\[dq] /etc/ | grep -v \[aq]#\[aq]  # exclude comments
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Shows exact lines and files referencing a network, useful for tracing
network origins or changes.
.PP
\f[B]Verify network parsing works correctly:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Test file with various formats
cat > test_networks.txt <<\[aq]EOF\[aq]
192.0.2.0/24
192.0.3.0 255.255.255.0
192.0.4.0-192.0.4.255
192.0.5.1
EOF

# Test each format
grepnet --extract --format cidr test_networks.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Validates that all supported formats are correctly parsed and
normalized.
.PP
\f[B]Check impact of comment filtering:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Count with comments included
COUNT_WITH=$(grepnet --no-filter-comments --extract config.txt | wc -l)

# Count with comments filtered (default)
COUNT_WITHOUT=$(grepnet --extract config.txt | wc -l)

echo \[dq]With comments: $COUNT_WITH\[dq]
echo \[dq]Without comments: $COUNT_WITHOUT\[dq]
echo \[dq]Networks in comments: $((COUNT_WITH - COUNT_WITHOUT))\[dq]
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Quantifies impact of commented network definitions on inventory.
.PP
\f[B]Test strict validation modes:\f[R]
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
# Create test file with mixed networks
echo \[aq]192.0.2.0/24
192.0.0.0/16
192.0.2.128/25\[aq] > test.txt

# Default exclude (subset)
echo \[dq]=== Default subset behavior ===\[dq]
grepnet --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] test.txt

# Strict subset (exact match only)
echo \[dq]=== Strict subset behavior ===\[dq]
grepnet --exclude-cidr-behaviour subset-strict --exclude-cidr \[aq]192.0.0.0/16\[aq] \[aq]0.0.0.0/0\[aq] test.txt
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Compares behaviors to understand filtering semantics.
.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES
.TP
\f[B]GREPNET_COLORS\f[R]
Customize output colors (space-separated \f[V]KEY=ANSI_CODE\f[R] pairs)
.PP
Supported color keys:
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]fn\f[R]: Filename (default: 35 = magenta)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]ln\f[R]: Line number (default: 32 = green)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]bn\f[R]: Byte offset (default: 32 = green)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]se\f[R]: Separator (default: 36 = cyan)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]sl\f[R]: Selected line background (default: 0 = none)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]cx\f[R]: Context line (default: 0 = none)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mt\f[R]: Match text color (default: 0 = none)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]ms\f[R]: Match separator (default: 0 = none)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mc\f[R]: Match colon (default: 0 = none)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]me\f[R]: EQUALS match type (default: 01;32 = bright green)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mb\f[R]: SUBSET match type (default: 01;34 = bright blue)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mS\f[R]: SUPERSET match type (default: 01;33 = bright yellow)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mo\f[R]: OVERLAP match type (default: 01;31 = bright red)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mu\f[R]: UNMATCHED type (default: 0 = none)
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[V]mn\f[R]: Normalized CIDR (default: 04 = underline)
.PP
ANSI codes are SGR (Select Graphic Rendition) values.
Common values: - \f[V]0\f[R] = reset/no color - \f[V]01\f[R] = bold -
\f[V]04\f[R] = underline - \f[V]31-37\f[R] = foreground colors (red,
green, yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, white) - Combine with semicolons:
\f[V]01;32\f[R] = bold green
.PP
\f[B]Example\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
export GREPNET_COLORS=\[dq]fn=35 me=01;31 mb=01;36\[dq]
grepnet \[aq]192.0.2.0/24\[aq] *.txt
\f[R]
.fi
